00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:44.100
A quick warning there will be spoilers in
this video, so just be aware of that or you

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.569
can watch my spoiler-free review here and
come back after you've seen the movie.

00:00:48.569 --> 00:00:52.039
When I interviewed Beauty And The Beast director
Bill Condon, he told me all about a funny

00:00:52.039 --> 00:00:56.409
deleted scene that involved LeFou and a new
enchanted character called Monsieur Toilette

00:00:56.409 --> 00:00:59.960
and which took place during the battle between
the enchanted objects and the villagers at

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:00.960
the Beast's castle.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.050
If you recall, leading up to the battle, LeFou
had already begun to question his allegiance

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:09.390
to Gaston, and in the deleted scene he opens
one of the castle's doors to hide away from

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:10.570
the fighting.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:15.250
Unfortunately for LeFou, behind this particular
door is an enchanted bathroom, and Monsieur

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.409
Toilette attacks him, drenching him with water.

00:01:18.409 --> 00:01:22.140
Later when the household objects change back
to human form, Monsieur Toilette, who is played

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.470
by Stephen Merchant, feels disgusted that
he's had to spend 10 years as an enchanted

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:30.479
toilet and he shares another funny moment
with Josh Gad's LeFou.

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:34.920
Bill Condon explained that the Monsieur Toilette
scene was cut for time and also because the

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.840
humour was considered a bit low-brow in comparison
to the rest of the scene.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:43.189
The battle scene also had other moments cut,
including one where the enchanted stove attacks

00:01:43.189 --> 00:01:47.420
the Village Lasses, which is what the Bimbettes
were called in the live-action movie.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:52.070
And just before the battle, Gaston rallies
the villagers with the rousing Mob Song.

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:55.619
As well some altered lyrics, you might have
noticed that there's a section of the song

00:01:55.619 --> 00:01:57.440
missing from the theatrical release.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.289
You can listen to the missing lyrics by Luke
Evans, Josh Gad and the villagers on the official

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:04.749
soundtrack, but the deleted section is from
a scene on the way to the castle where the

00:02:04.749 --> 00:02:08.479
mob creep past the wolves singing, "We don't
like what we don't understand.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:10.080
In fact, it scares us.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.489
And this monster is mysterious at least."

00:02:12.489 --> 00:02:16.269
Bill Condon was sad to cut this scene as it
was so beautiful, but explained that it ended

00:02:16.269 --> 00:02:20.550
up downplaying the tension of the moment and
was also cut for time.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:24.730
Given how incredibly popular Disney's animated
Beauty And The Beast movie is and the fact

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:28.870
it won two Oscars for its music, it seems
unbelievable that early in development the

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:32.720
studio wanted to completely overhaul the story
and cut out all the songs.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.630
Apparently, at the time, the studio wanted
to go in a similar direction to the 2012 movie

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:40.739
Snow White And The Huntsman, which starred
Kristen Stewart.

00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:45.890
It wasn't until the huge success of Frozen
in 2013 that Disney realised there was enormous

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:51.140
potential in a musical remake, however, the
studio was still unsure and wanted to just

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.560
do half the songs from the original movie.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:58.050
Director Bill Condon, who has a strong musical
background with films like Chicago and Dreamgirls,

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:01.860
refused to take on the project until Disney
recognised that a full-on musical was the

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.019
best approach.

00:03:03.019 --> 00:03:08.450
I absolutely love Alan Menken and Howard Ashman's
songs from the 1991 film, so I'm really glad

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:12.239
that the studio did come to its senses and
go for a proper musical movie in the end,

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:15.439
and even added some new songs by Menken and
Tim Rice.

00:03:15.439 --> 00:03:19.000
Speaking of music and live-action remakes
of Disney movies, it'll be interesting to

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:23.890
see just how much music actually makes it
into the live-action version of Mulan as director

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:29.120
Niki Caro first said there wouldn't be any
songs, but later said there will be music.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:32.590
When I interviewed Emma Watson and Dan Stevens
about the movie, they explained that there

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:36.980
were plans for a scene involving Belle where
she convinced Beast to convert his vast personal

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:40.410
library into a public space or public library.

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:43.800
Belle wanted to open up the knowledge to the
whole world and it was also going to be a

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.050
place where she would combine her passion
for books and teaching and educating others,

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:50.540
much as we see her do at the beginning of
the movie.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:54.829
Emma Watson said she was gutted when the public
library idea didn't make it into the final

00:03:54.829 --> 00:03:59.620
film, but according to Dan Stevens there wasn't
room for it in the shape of the final story.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:03.579
There's also a picture of a deleted scene
between Belle and the beggar woman Agathe,

00:04:03.579 --> 00:04:07.739
who's the Enchantress in disguise and this
scene looks like it probably happened early

00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:08.739
in the movie.

00:04:08.739 --> 00:04:12.600
By the way, if you'd like to discover all
the secrets of the new live-action Enchantress,

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.750
then tap in the top right of the screen to
check out my video.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:20.229
There's a wonderful moment in the original
movie where LeFou gets covered in snow like

00:04:20.229 --> 00:04:22.330
a snowman outside Belle's house.

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:27.270
Josh Gad, who plays LeFou and is the voice
of Olaf the snowman in Frozen, says he begged

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:31.680
Bill Condon to include the scene in the live-action
movie as it's such a great easter egg and

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.280
in-joke for Disney fans.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.199
Sadly, the scene never made it into the final
film, perhaps because it wouldn't have fit

00:04:37.199 --> 00:04:40.930
in the new version because there wasn't any
need for LeFou to spy on Belle's house as

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:42.940
he did in the animation.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:47.280
In the final transformation scene where Beast
turns back into the prince, Dan Stevens reportedly

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.889
filmed scenes where he was shirtless and rose
up from a layer of rose petals.

00:04:50.889 --> 00:04:54.540
Rather different then to his shirtless scene
in the horror-thriller The Guest.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.160
During Beauty And The Beast test screenings,
though, it was felt that the way the scene

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:01.350
was shot made it look like Dan Stevens was
perhaps fully naked, so the scene ended up

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:02.800
being cut.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:06.720
And Luke Evans explained that the moment in
the original animation where Gaston reveals

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.740
that "every last inch of [him's] covered with
hair" was also cut because it wasn't considered

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:11.960
family friendly enough.

